WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.660
Speaker 1: We have some good but potentially
temporary news, depending on who the next

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:11.320
president is, and that news is that President
Joe Biden has suspended drilling leases in

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.780
the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, also
known as ANWR.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.779
The decision blocks for now oil and gas drilling
in one of the largest tracts of undeveloped

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:23.560
wilderness in the United States.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.210
The Biden administration yesterday suspended
oil drilling leases in ANWR.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.669
Unschooling, a signature achievement of the
Trump presidency.

00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:31.900
That's a good thing.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.650
And delivering on a promise by President Biden
to protect the fragile Alaskan tundra from

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.310
fossil fuel extraction.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:42.190
The decision sets up a process that could
halt drilling in one of the largest tracts

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:48.070
of untouched wilderness in the US home to
migrating waterfowl Cariboo and polar bears.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:51.510
But it also lives over as much as 11 billion
barrels of oil.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:55.020
And Democrats and Republicans have fought
over whether to allow drilling there for more

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.410
than four decades.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:02.270
A former formal order from Secretary Interior
Secretary Deb Halland paused the leases until

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:07.780
her agency has completed an environmental
analysis of their impact and a legal review

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:12.310
of the Trump administration's decision to
grant them.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.229
So this is good.

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:16.420
This is this is what could be done right now.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.060
We're four months in or but yeah, four months
and almost five, four and a half months into

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.179
Joe Biden's presidency.

00:01:22.179 --> 00:01:24.670
But this really needs to be made permanent.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.850
And the point that I keep going back to on
this is we really don't need this.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:34.749
And, of course, you know, the right wingers,
the gas price will go up a tenth of a percent

00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:37.240
again and they'll point to this.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:45.459
I actually saw a comment on Reddit which said
nothing like importing foreign oil at increased

00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:52.009
rates just so that later we can go and drill
the very oil that we are now protecting.

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:54.560
No one is thinking long term.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.399
That is such an incorrect perspective.

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:03.579
The whole point here is we should be moving
away from oil.

00:02:03.579 --> 00:02:08.580
Anybody who's saying, you know, the goal for
energy should be let's keep pushing, even

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.240
if it takes years to be drilling in the Arctic
and the ANWR refuge.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.140
No, no, no, no, no.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.570
We we should be using this time during which
we actually already have plenty of oil to

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.580
transition away from oil.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:28.890
And I think that very often this debate gets
into these strawman arguments like, listen,

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.790
if you shut off oil today, heat, indoor heating
goes away.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.280
We don't have gasoline for vehicles.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.280
We don't have this.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.280
We don't have that.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.170
I don't know who they're referring to when
they say that.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:47.530
But I've never met anyone serious on the left
who says we should be off oil today.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:51.840
I understand that if we there would be no
way to get off of oil today.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.000
But the whole point here is we should be dramatically
accelerating solar power development.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.770
Oh, but, David, there's not you can't use
solar power to generate.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:05.890
Well, look at the efficiency increase we've
seen in solar panels over the last 20 years.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:11.800
And if we get even half of the same over the
next 10, that goes a huge way in combination

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.590
with wind and geothermal, etc..

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.140
Now, there is a real conversation to have
about nuclear.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:23.270
I've said before, new nuclear power is very,
very safe.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:29.010
I am not a reflexive anti-nuclear activist
very often.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:34.080
And we've talked to nuclear engineers on the
nuclear energy engineers on the show.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.640
Very often there's this reflexive oh, but
look at look at Chernobyl and look at this

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.320
stuff from from the 70s.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:49.690
The reality is that if we were to bring on
new nuclear today with modern nuclear technology,

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.740
none of those things would be issues.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:57.500
So for me, I'm totally comfortable using new
nuclear as a stop gap.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.160
What are the concerns that I have with it?

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:07.330
Number one, the disposal of the waste continues
to be an issue I don't know about, you know,

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.040
but there's people who say now you just ship
it, you put it underground is fine.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.319
I'm just not sure about that.

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:15.950
I'm not saying there isn't a solution, but
that still remains a question for me.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:21.019
But really, the bigger question for me with
saying let's use nuclear as a way to be a

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:28.400
bridge off of fossil fuels and onto real renewables,
is that it seems to me we would get addicted

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.640
to nuclear and it takes a while to bring new
nuclear technology online.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.000
So my concern would be if we say let's do
new nuclear and it'll take eight years to

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.320
bring some of this online, by the time that
it's online, the fact that we've been focusing

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:50.790
on nuclear might have diverted energy and
attention and funding from get from real renewables.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.000
And that scares me.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.420
But in theory, I am not against doing a new
safe nuclear as a stopgap.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:04.460
One other thing, there are people who like
to say the sun's not really renewable, you

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:11.740
know, I mean, we've got five to six billion
years of solar, OK, that's if we buy if the

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:16.350
human race survives that long, we can never
be certain about anything.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:22.100
But I would be reasonably sure that we would
have moved somewhere where the sun exploding

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.900
in five to six billion years is not going
to be what destroys the human race.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:32.070
So for all practical purposes, when we're
talking about peak oil and we're talking about

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:37.320
10, 20, 50, 100 year timelines of what we're
doing to the planet with oil use, five to

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.610
six billion years of solar power sounds pretty
damn renewable to me.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:41.610
All right.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.480
So good decision by Joe Biden will follow
it.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.051
And of course, there's a bigger conversation
here about a bigger transition that needs

00:05:48.051 --> 00:05:48.062
to take plac

